Bring-up modules from the Pixel 9a's SLEEP-PLAN S1, "What is left" item 1
(omarchy-mobile, device/tegu/SLEEP-PLAN.md), 2026-11-12 and 11-13: the sleep
that never woke, which is a register access to a USI right after its clock
gates are opened (the fix is linux-omarchy-mobile#247). Not for merge; built
out of tree against the kernel under test:

  make -C <tree> O=<out> ARCH=arm64 LLVM=1 M=$PWD modules

Neither runs on the palmtop again before an attended run with the owner's
yes (omarchy-mobile AGENTS.md, the 9a sleep entry). The first runs of both
did not have one.

tegu-hsi0-usi-clk-hold.c  takes a reference on pclk and ipclk of the USIs at
                   111d00c0 and 111e00c0 (HSI0's USI2 and USI3) while
                   loaded, so the clock framework never closes their gates;
                   ip=0 holds pclk alone. Reads and writes no register of
                   its own. With it loaded, 300 of 300 sleeps woke on the
                   build that had hung at the 70th and the 41st
                   (push1-dbg-gc63fa9271bb8, 2026-11-12). With #247 in the
                   kernel it changes nothing.
tegu-hsi0-usi-stress.c  repeats exynos_usi_enable()'s sequence on one USI
                   from a thread bound to a CPU: both clocks on, USI_CON
                   read and written back with the reset bit clear (what the
                   driver writes at every resume), 1 us, both clocks off.
                   IT HANGS THE SOC ON PURPOSE on a kernel whose USI gates
                   are switched: on the build with only the PCLK gates
                   critical (push1-dbg-g9af120756764), usi=3 with no wait
                   hung within the first 1,000 rounds, twice of twice, and
                   the cluster watchdog reset the palmtop about 40 s later
                   (systemd's runtime watchdog was feeding it; without a
                   fed watchdog it would wait for the buttons). access=0
                   (gates switched, no register touched) and delay_us=10
                   (10 us between the clocks and the access) each ran
                   2,000,000 rounds with no hang. On a kernel with #247 the
                   gates never close and 2,000,000 rounds passed on each
                   USI. Run it only with the modem off (nothing else uses
                   USI3 then), the filesystems synced, a watchdog fed from
                   userspace, and never with usi=2 while the touchscreen is
                   in use.

    insmod tegu-hsi0-usi-stress.ko loops=2000000 report=1000 usi=3 cpu=7 \
           [access=0] [delay_us=10]
